It was late in the day on Oct. 27, 2021, when Fred Ruckel received the dreaded automated email from Amazon . Amazon’s software had detected that Ruckel’s popular cat toy, called the Ripple Rug, was being sold somewhere else for a cheaper price. His product would no longer be shown in Amazon’s all-important buy box, an area of the listing where shoppers click “Add to Cart.” Ruckel is the sole seller of the Ripple Rug on Amazon, so the move all but ensured his product would disappear from the website, costing him thousands of dollars per day.","entities":["Fred Ruckel","Eastvale","Watchara Phomicinda","MediaNews Group","The Riverside Press-Enterprise","Amazon fulfillment center","Ripple Rug","FTC antitrust case","Amazon sellers sound off on the FTC's 'long-overdue' antitrust case","Getty Images","California","FTC","CNBC","Amazon"],"facts":["Amazon sellers sound off on the FTC's 'long-overdue' antitrust case -MENTIONS-> Federal Trade Commission","Amazon sellers sound off on the FTC's 'long-overdue' antitrust case -MENTIONS-> Snuggly Cat","Snuggly Cat -MADE-> Ripple Rug","Zapolsky -SAID-> FTC antitrust case","Which M.L.B. Playoff Team Has the Most Daunting Home Field? We Ranked Them. -MENTIONS-> Getty Images"]}

